Genealogy of Jesus Christ
<unused86> The book of the generation of Jesus Christ, the son of David, the son of Abraham:
Ibrahim, son of Isaac. And Isaac, son of Jacob. And Jacob, son of Judah and his brothers.
And Judah fathered Perez and Zerah by Tamar. And Perez fathered Hezron. And Hezron fathered Aram.
And Aram was the father of Aminadab. And Aminadab was the father of Nahshon. And Nahshon was the father of Salmon.
And Salmon fathered Boaz by Rahab, and Boaz fathered Obed by Ruth, and Obed fathered Jesse.
And Jesse fathered David the king. And David the king fathered Solomon by the one who had been Uriah’s wife.
And Solomon was the son of Rehoboam. And Rehoboam was the son of Abijah. And Abijah was the son of Asa.
And Asa begot Jehoshaphat. And Jehoshaphat begot Joram. And Joram begot Uzziah.
And Uzziah begot Jotham. And Jotham begot Ahaz. And Ahaz begot Hezekiah.
And Hezekiah begot Manasseh. And Manasseh begot Amon. And Amon begot Josiah.
And Josiah was the son of Jehoiakim, and his brothers, at the time of the Babylonian captivity.
And after the captivity of Babylon, Kenaniah begat Shealtiel. And Shealtiel begat Zerubbabel.
And Zerubbabel begat Abiud. And Abiud begat Eliakim. And Eliakim begat Azor.
And Azor was the son of Sadok. And Sadok was the son of Achim. And Achim was the son of Eliud.
And Eliud begot Eleazar. And Eleazar begot Matthan. And Matthan begot Jacob.
And Jacob was the father of Joseph, the husband of Mary, of whom Jesus was born, who is called Christ.
And all the generations from Abraham to David were fourteen generations, and from David to the exile to Babylon fourteen generations, and from the exile to Babylon to the Christ fourteen generations. The birth of Jesus Christ
Now the birth of Jesus Christ was as follows: When Mary his mother was betrothed to Joseph, before they came together, she was found to be with child from the Holy Spirit.
And Joseph, being a righteous man, did not want to expose her to public disgrace. He intended to divorce her quietly.
But while he thought on these things, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared unto him in a dream, saying, O Joseph, son of David, fear not to take unto thee Mary thy wife: for that which is conceived in her is of the Holy Ghost.
And she will bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus. For he will save his people from their sins.”
And all this was to fulfill what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying:
Behold, a virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and they shall call his name Emmanuel, which means, “God with us.”
And when Joseph awoke from his sleep, he did as the angel of the Lord commanded him and took his wife.
And he did not know her until she gave birth to her firstborn son. And they called his name Jesus.
The Visit of the Magi
Now when Jesus was born in Bethlehem of Judaea in the days of Herod the king, behold, wise men from the east came to Jerusalem.
saying: “Where is the newborn King of the Jews? For we have seen his star in the east, and have come to worship him.”
When Herod the king heard this, he was greatly disturbed, and all Jerusalem with him.
So he gathered all the chief priests and scribes of the people and asked them, “Where is the Messiah to be born?”
And they said to him, “In Bethlehem of Judea, for thus it is written by the prophet:
And you, Bethlehem, land of Judah, are by no means least among the cities of Judah, for from you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel.”
Then Herod secretly called the magi and learned from them the time when the star had appeared.
Then he sent them to Bethlehem, saying, “Go and search diligently for the child. And when you have found him, let me know, that I too may come and worship him.”
And when they heard from the king, they went. And behold, the star which they had seen in the east came and stood over where the young child was.
And when they saw the star, they rejoiced with great joy.
And they came to the house and saw the child with Mary, his mother. They fell down and worshiped him. Then they opened their treasures and presented him with gifts: gold, frankincense, and myrrh.
Then, when they had been warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they departed for their own country by another way. The Flight to Egypt
And after they had departed, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream, saying, “Rise, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt, and remain there until I tell you, for Herod is about to seek the child to destroy him.”
So he arose and took the boy and his mother by night and departed to Egypt.
And so fulfilled what was spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying, “Out of Egypt I called my son.”
Then Herod, when he saw that he had been tricked by the magi, became very angry. And he sent and slew all the male children in Bethlehem and in all its borders, those who were two years old and under, according to the time which he had ascertained from the magi.
Then was fulfilled what was spoken by the prophet Jeremiah, saying:
A sound was heard in Ramah, weeping and lamentation and much grief. Rachel is weeping for her children and refuses to be comforted, because they are no more. Back to Nazareth.
Now when Herod was dead, behold, an angel of the Lord appeared in a dream to Joseph in Egypt.
saying: “Get up, take the boy and his mother, and go to the land of Israel, for those who were seeking the boy’s life are dead.”
So he got up and took the boy and his mother and went to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning over Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And having been warned in a dream, he withdrew to the regions of Galilee.
And he came and lived in a city called Nazareth, that what was spoken by the prophets might be fulfilled: “He shall be called a Nazarene.”
John the Baptist prepares the way.
And in those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
saying, “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.”
For this is the one about whom Isaiah the prophet spoke, saying, “A voice of one crying in the wilderness: Prepare the way of the Lord, make his paths straight.”
And John himself was clothed in camel’s hair, with a leather belt around his waist. And his food was locusts and wild honey.
Then Jerusalem went out to him, and all Judea, and all the region around the Jordan,
And they accepted from him in Jordan, confessing their sins.
When he saw many of the Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his baptism, he said to them, “You brood of vipers, who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?”
So produce fruits worthy of repentance.
And do not think to yourselves, “Abraham is our father.” For I say to you that God is able to raise children to Abraham from these stones.
And now the ax is laid at the root of the trees, so every tree that does not produce good fruit will be cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
He winnows with a fork in his hand, and thoroughly cleanses his threshing floor, and gathers his wheat into the granary, but the chaff he burns with unquenchable fire.” The baptism of Jesus Christ.
Then Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan to John, to be baptized by him.
But John tried to prevent him, saying, “I need to be baptized by you, and you come to me?”
Then Jesus answered and said to him, “Permit it for now, for thus it is fitting for us to fulfill all righteousness.” Then he allowed it.
And as Jesus was coming up out of the water, the heavens opened, and He saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and coming to rest on Him.
And a voice from heaven said, “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.”
Jesus Faces Temptation
Then Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.
And after fasting for forty days and forty nights, he finally became hungry.
Then the tempter came to him and said, “If you are the Son of God, tell these stones to become bread.”
And he answered and said, “It is written: ‘Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God.’”
Then he took him to the holy city and placed him on the pinnacle of the temple,
And he said to him, “If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down, for it is written: ‘He will command his angels concerning you, and they will lift you up in their hands, so that you will not strike your foot against a stone.’”
And Jesus said to him, “It is also written, ‘You shall not put the Lord your God to the test.’”
Then he also took him to a very high mountain, and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their glory.
And he said to him, “All this I will give you if you fall down and worship me.”
Then Jesus said to him, “Get away, Satan! For it is written: ‘You shall worship the Lord your God, and him alone you shall serve.’”
Then the devil left him, and behold, angels came and were ministering to him. The beginning of his ministry was in Galilee.
And when Jesus heard that John was delivered up, he departed unto Galilee.
And he left Nazareth and came and lived in Capernaum, which is by the sea, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali,
That what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled:
Land of Zebulun, land of Naphtali, the way of the sea, beyond the Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles.
The people sitting in darkness have seen a great light, and those sitting in the land of death and the shadow of death, a light has dawned upon them.
From that time Jesus began to preach, saying, “Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand.” The calling of the first disciples.
And as Jesus walked by the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers, Simon called Peter, and Andrew his brother, casting a net into the sea, for they were fishermen.
Then He said to them, “Follow Me, and I will make you fishers of men.”
So he left the window and followed them.
Then He passed by there and saw two other brothers, James son of Zebedee and his brother John, in the boat with their father Zebedee, mending their nets. And He called them.
So they left the ship and their father and followed Him. Jesus teaches and heals the sick.
And Jesus went about all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, and preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing all manner of sickness and all manner of disease among the people.
And his fame spread throughout Syria. So they brought to him all the sick afflicted with various diseases and pains, the insane, the epileptic, and the paralyzed, and he healed them.
And large crowds followed Him from Galilee and Decapolis and Jerusalem and Judea and beyond the Jordan.
The Sermon on the Mount - The Beatitudes
And when He saw the crowds, He went up on the mountain, and when He was seated, His disciples came to Him.
So he opened his mouth and taught them, saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they shall be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they will inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be filled.
Blessed are the merciful, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they will see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they will be called sons of God.
Blessed are those who are persecuted for righteousness’ sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people insult you and persecute you and falsely say all kinds of evil against you because of me.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward is great in heaven, for so they persecuted the prophets who were before you. You are the salt of the earth, and the light of the world.
You are the salt of the earth, but if the salt loses its flavor, how shall it be seasoned? It is no longer good for anything, except to be thrown out and trampled underfoot.
You are the light of the world. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden.
And they do not light a lamp and put it under a basket, but on a lampstand, so that it shines for all those in the house.
Let your light so shine before men, that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father which is in heaven. Fulfillment of the Law.
Do not think that I have come to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I have not come to abolish them but to fulfill them.
Verily I say unto you, Till heaven and earth pass, one jot or one tittle shall in no wise pass from the law, till all be fulfilled.
Therefore, whoever breaks one of these minor commandments and teaches others to do so will be called the least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever does and teaches, this one will be called great in the kingdom of heaven.
So I say to you that unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will not enter the kingdom of heaven. Wrath.
You have heard that it was said to those of old, “You shall not murder, and whoever murders will be liable to judgment.”
But I say to you that everyone who is angry with his brother without cause will be liable to judgment, and whoever says to his brother, ‘Raca,’ will be liable to the council, and whoever says, ‘You fool,’ will be liable to the fire of hell.
So if you are presenting your offering at the altar, and there you remember that your brother has something against you,
Therefore leave your gift there before the altar, and go first and be reconciled to your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Be reconciled with your adversary quickly while you are with him on the way, lest your adversary deliver you to the judge, and the judge deliver you to the officer, and you be thrown into prison.
Truly I tell you, do not leave there until you have paid the very last penny! Adultery.
You have heard that it was said to those of old, “You shall not commit adultery.”
And as for me, I say to you that everyone who looks at a woman with lust has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
And if your right eye causes you to stumble, pluck it out and cast it from you, for it is better for you that one of your members perish than for your whole body to be cast into hell.
And if your right hand causes you to stumble, cut it off and throw it away. For it is better for you to lose one of your members than for your whole body to be thrown into hell. Divorce.
And it was said: Whoever divorces his wife should give her a divorce document.
And as for me, I say to you that whoever divorces his wife except for the reason of adultery causes her to commit adultery, and whoever marries a divorced woman commits adultery. By oath.
You also heard that it was said to those of old, ‘Do not swear falsely, but fulfill your vows to the Lord.’
And as for me, I say to you, do not swear at all, not by heaven, for it is the throne of God,
Nor by the earth, for it is His footstool, nor by Jerusalem, for it is the city of the great King.
And do not swear by your head, for you cannot make one hair white or black.
But let your speech be: Yes, yes, no, no. And whatever is more than that is from the evil one. Vengeance.
You have heard that it was said: an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth.
But I tell you, do not resist an evil person. On the contrary, if anyone slaps you on the right cheek, turn the other also.
And if anyone wants to sue you and take your shirt, give him the coat as well.
And if someone mocks you with a slight, respond with two.
Whoever asks of you, give to him, and whoever wants to borrow from you, do not turn him away. Loving your enemies.
You have heard that it was said, “Love your neighbor and hate your enemy.”
But I tell you, love your enemies. Bless those who curse you. Do good to those who hate you, and pray for those who mistreat you and persecute you.
So that you may be children of your Father who is in heaven, for He causes His sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
If you love those who love you, what reward do you have? Do not even tax collectors do the same?
And if you greet only your brothers, what credit is that to you? Do not even the Gentiles do the same?
Therefore be perfect, as your Father who is in heaven is perfect.
Charity
Beware of practicing your righteousness before other people to be seen by them. Otherwise, you have no reward with your Father who is in heaven.
So when you give to the needy, do not announce it with trumpets before you, as the hypocrites do in the synagogues and in the streets, to be praised by men. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward!
And as for you, when you give charity, do not let your left hand know what your right hand is doing.
So that your charity may be in secret. For your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly. Prayer.
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites, for they love to pray standing in the synagogues and at the corners of the streets, that they may be seen by men. Truly, I say to you, they have received their reward!
And when you pray, go into your room and shut the door, and pray to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you publicly.
And when you pray, do not repeat empty phrases as the Gentiles do, for they think that by their many words they will be heard.
Do not be like them. For your Father knows what you need before you ask Him.
So pray like this: Our Father who is in heaven, hallowed be your name.
Your kingdom come. Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.
Our bread for today give us.
And forgive us our sins, as we forgive those who sin against us.
And do not put us to the test, but deliver us from evil. For Yours is the kingdom, and the power, and the glory, forever. Amen.
For if you forgive others their trespasses, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.
If you do not forgive others for their slips, your Father will not forgive your slips either. Fasting.
And when you fast, do not look gloomy like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces that they may appear to men to be fasting. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward.
And as for you, when you are silent, oil your head and wash your face,
So that you may not display your fasting to men, but to your Father who is in secret. And your Father who sees in secret will reward you openly. Treasures in heaven.
Do not store up for yourselves treasures on earth, where moths and rust destroy, and where thieves break in and steal.
But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moths and rust do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in and steal.
For where your treasure is, there your heart will be also.
The lamp of the body is the eye. If your eye is healthy, your whole body will be full of light.
If your eye is evil, your whole body will be full of darkness. If the light within you is darkness, how great will the darkness be!
No one can serve two masters, for he will either hate the one and love the other, or be devoted to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and wealth. God takes care of us.
Therefore I tell you, do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink; nor about your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothes?
Look at the birds of the sky: they do not sow or reap or gather into barns, yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not much more valuable than they?
And who among you, if he worries, can add a cubit to his height?
And why do you care about clothing? Consider how the lilies of the field grow! They do not toil nor do they spin.
But I say to you that even Solomon in all his glory was not arrayed like one of these.
If He so clothes the grass of the field, which is here today and tomorrow is thrown into the oven, will He not much more clothe you, O you of little faith?
So do not worry, saying: What shall we eat? Or what shall we drink? Or what shall we wear?
For all these things the nations seek. For your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things.
But seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be added to you.
Therefore do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about its own things. Enough for the day is its own trouble.
Condemning others
Do not judge, or you too will be judged.
For with the judgment you pronounce, you will be judged, and with the measure you use, it will be measured back to you.
And why do you look at the speck in your brother’s eye, but do not notice the log that is in your own eye?
Or how will you say to your brother, ‘Let me remove the speck from your eye,’ while the log is in your own eye?
You hypocrite, first remove the beam from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother’s eye!
Do not give what is holy to dogs, nor cast your pearls before pigs, lest they trample them underfoot and turn to attack you. Ask, seek, knock.
Ask and it will be given to you. Seek and you will find. Knock and the door will be opened to you.
For everyone who asks receives, and he who seeks finds, and to him who knocks it will be opened.
Or which of you, if your son asks for bread, would give him a stone?
And if he is asked for a fish, will he give him a snake?
If you, then, though you are evil, know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask him!
And as you wish that men would do to you, do so to them; for this is the law and the prophets. The narrow gate.
Enter through the narrow gate. For the gate is wide and the way is easy that leads to destruction, and those who enter by it are many.
How narrow the gate and constricted the road that leads to life, and few are those who find it! The tree and its fruit.
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing, but inwardly are ravenous wolves!
By their fruits you will recognize them. Do people pick grapes from thorn bushes, or figs from thistles?
Thus, every good tree produces good fruit, but the bad tree produces bad fruit.
A good tree cannot produce bad fruit, nor can a bad tree produce good fruit.
Every tree that does not produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
By their fruits you will know them.
Not everyone who says to me, ‘Lord, Lord!’ will enter the kingdom of heaven, but the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to Me on that day, ‘Lord, Lord! Did we not prophesy in Your name, and cast out demons in Your name, and do many miracles in Your name?’
Then I will declare to them, ‘I never knew you!’ Depart from me, you workers of iniquity! The wise builders and the foolish builders.
And everyone who hears these words of mine and acts on them will be like a wise man who built his house on the rock.
And the rain came, and the rivers came, and the winds blew and beat upon that house, and it did not fall, because it was founded on the rock.
And whoever hears these words of mine and does not act upon them, is like a foolish man who built his house on the sand.
So the rain fell, and the rivers came, and the winds blew and struck that house, and it fell, and its fall was great!
Now when Jesus had finished these sayings, the crowds were astonished at his teaching,
For He was teaching them as one having authority, and not as the scribes.
Healing of a leper.
And when he descended from the mountain, a large crowd followed him.
And a leper came to Him, bowing down to Him, and saying, “Lord, if You will, You can make me clean.”
And Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, “I will; be clean!” Immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
And Jesus said to him, “See that you tell no one, but go, show yourself to the priest, and offer the sacrifice that Moses commanded for your testimony.” The centurion’s faith.
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a centurion came to him asking him
And he says: “O master, my servant lies in the house paralyzed and in severe torment.”
And Jesus said to him, “I am coming and will heal him.”
And the centurion replied, “Lord, I am not worthy to have you come under my roof, but just say the word, and my servant will be healed.”
For I too am a man under authority. I have soldiers under my command. I say to one, ‘Go!’ and he goes, and to another, ‘Come!’ and he comes, and to my servant, ‘Do this!’ and he does.”
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who followed him, “Truly I tell you, I have not found such faith even in Israel!”
And I say to you that many will come from the east and the west and recline with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of the heavens.
“But the children of the kingdom will be thrown into the outer darkness. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”
Then Jesus said to the centurion, “Go, and as you have believed, it will be done for you.” And his servant was healed in that hour. Healing Peter’s mother-in-law and others.
And when Jesus came into Peter’s house, he saw his mother-in-law lying sick with a fever,
So he touched her hand, and the fever left her, and she got up and served them.
And when evening came, many demon-possessed people were brought to Him, and He cast out the spirits with a word, and healed all who were sick.
In order that what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled, saying: “He Himself took our infirmities and carried our diseases.” The price of obedience.
And when Jesus saw large crowds around Him, He gave the order to go to the other side.
Then a scribe came forward and said to Him, “Teacher, I will follow You wherever You go.”
And Jesus said to him, “The foxes have holes and the birds of the air have nests, but the Son of Man has nowhere to lay his head.”
And another of his disciples said to him, “Lord, let me go and bury my father first.”
And Jesus said to him, “Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead.” Calming the storm.
And when He got into the boat, His disciples followed Him.
And a great storm arose on the sea, so that the waves crashed over the ship, and he was asleep.
Then his disciples came and woke him, saying, “Lord, save us! We are perishing!”
Then he said to them, “Why are you afraid, you of little faith?” And he rose and rebuked the winds and the sea; and there was a great calm.
Then the people were amazed, saying, “Who is this, that even the wind and the sea obey him?” He drove out demons.
And when He came to Gerasa, a country of the Gadarenes, two demon-possessed men met Him, coming out of the tombs, exceedingly fierce, so that no one could pass by that way.
And when they cried out, they said, “What have you to do with us, Jesus, Son of God? Have you come here to torment us before the time?”
And a large herd of pigs was grazing far from them.
Then the demons implored Him, saying, “If You cast us out, permit us to go away into the herd of pigs.”
So he said to them, “Go.” They went and drove the herd of pigs into the sea, and the whole herd of pigs plunged from the cliff into the sea and drowned in the water.
But the shepherds fled and went to the city, and they told everything that had happened, and about the two demon-possessed men.
So the whole city went out to meet Jesus. And when they saw Him, they begged Him to depart from their region.
Healing of a paralyzed person.
So he entered the ship and set sail, and came to his city.
And when they brought a paralytic to Him, lying on a bed, Jesus saw their faith and said to the paralytic, “Son, your sins are forgiven.”
And some of the scribes said to themselves, “This man is blaspheming!”
And Jesus, knowing their thoughts, said, “Why are you thinking evil in your hearts?”
Which is easier to say: “Your sins are forgiven,” or “Get up and walk”?
But that you may know that the Son of Man has power on earth to forgive sins.” Then He said to the paralytic, “Rise, take up your bed and go to your home!”
So he got up and went to his home.
And when the crowds saw it, they were amazed and glorified God, who had given people such authority. Matthew’s call.
And as Jesus passed on from there, He saw a man sitting at the tax office, named Matthew. And He said to him, “Follow Me.” And he rose and followed Him.
And while he was reclining in the house, behold, many tax collectors and sinners came and were reclining with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, “Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?”
When Jesus heard this, he said to them, “It is not the healthy who need a doctor, but the sick.”
“Go and learn what this means: ‘I desire mercy, not sacrifice.’ For I have not come to call the righteous but sinners to repentance.” Question about fasting.
Then came to Him the disciples of John, saying, “Why do we and the Pharisees fast often, but Your disciples do not fast?”
Then Jesus said to them, “Can the guests of the wedding mourn as long as the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them, and then they will fast.”
No one patches an old garment with a piece of new cloth, for the patch pulls away from the old, and a worse tear is made.
And they do not put new wine into old wineskins, lest the wineskins burst, and the wine spills, and the wineskins are ruined. But they put new wine into fresh wineskins, and both are preserved.” The raising of Jairus’s daughter and the healing of the woman with the hemorrhage.
And while he was saying these things, behold, a ruler came and knelt before Him, saying, “My daughter is dying; come and lay Your hand on her, and she will live.”
So Jesus got up and followed him, and his disciples.
And a woman who had been suffering from a hemorrhage for twelve years came up behind him and touched the fringe of his cloak.
for she said to herself, “If I only touch his garment, I will be made well.”
Then Jesus turned and saw her, and said, “Take heart, daughter, your faith has made you well.” And the woman was healed from that hour.
And when Jesus came into the house of the ruler, looking at them that wailed and the multitude that made a great uproar,
He said to them, “Move aside, for the girl is not dead but sleeping.” And they laughed at him.
And when the assembly departed, he entered and took her by the hand, and the young woman arose.
So the news went out to all that land. A healing for two blind men.
And as Jesus passed by, two blind men followed Him, crying out and saying, “Have mercy on us, Son of David!”
And when He came to the house, two blind men came to Him. And Jesus said to them, “Do you believe that I am able to do this?” They said to Him, “Yes, Lord!”
Then He touched their eyes, saying, “According to your faith let it be to you.”
Then their eyes were opened. And Jesus rebuked them, saying, “See that no one knows!”
But they went out and spread it throughout that land. A cure for the mute.
And as they were going out, a mute man possessed by a demon was brought to him.
And when the demon was cast out, the mute man spoke, and the crowd marveled, saying, “Nothing like this has ever appeared in Israel!”
Now the Pharisees said, “He casts out demons by the prince of demons!” Few deeds [are done].
And Jesus went through every city and village, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every disease and every weakness among the people.
And when He saw the crowds, He had compassion for them, because they were harassed and scattered like sheep without a shepherd.
Then He said to His disciples, “The harvest is plentiful but the workers are few.”
“Ask the Lord of the harvest to send laborers into his harvest.”
Sending the twelve
Then he called his twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits to cast them out, and to heal every disease and every weakness.
And as for the names of the twelve apostles, they are these: First, Simon, who is called Peter, and Andrew his brother. James the son of Zebedee, and John his brother.
Phillips and Bartholomew. Thomas, and Matthew the tax collector. James son of Halphas, and Thaddaeus, who is also known as Judas.
Simeon the Canaanite, and Judas Iscariot who betrayed him.
These twelve Jesus sent out and instructed them, saying: “Do not go on a road to the Gentiles, and do not enter a Samaritan town.
But go to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.
And as you go, proclaim, saying, ‘The kingdom of heaven is at hand.’
Heal the sick. Cleanse the lepers. Raise the dead. Cast out demons. You received without paying; give without pay.
Do not acquire gold or silver or copper in your regions,
nor a provision for the road, nor two tunics, nor sandals, nor a staff, for the worker deserves his food.
And whatever city or village you enter, search out those within who are worthy, and stay there until you depart.
And when you enter a house, greet those within it.
If the house is worthy, let your peace return to it; but if it is not worthy, let your peace return to you.
And whoever does not receive you or listen to your words, shake off the dust from your feet as you depart from that house or that city.
Truly I say to you, the land of Sodom and Gomorrah will have a more tolerable state in the day of judgment than that city.
Behold, I send you out as sheep in the midst of wolves. Be wise as serpents and harmless as doves.
But beware of people, for they will hand you over to courts, and in their synagogues they will flog you.
And you will be led before governors and kings for My sake, to bear witness to them and to the Gentiles.
So when they hand you over, do not worry about what or how you speak, for you are giving in that hour what you speak with.
For it is not you who are speaking, but the Spirit of your Father who is speaking through you.
And a brother will hand over his brother to death, and a father his son, and children will rise up against their parents and kill them.
and you will be hated by all for My name’s sake. But whoever perseveres to the end will be saved.
And when they drive you out of one city, flee to the next. For truly, I say to you, you will not have gone through the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than the teacher, nor is the servant better than his master.
It is enough for the student to be like his teacher, and the slave like his master. If they called the Lord of the house Beelzebul, how much more his household!
Do not fear them. For what is concealed will not be revealed, and what is hidden will not be known.
What I tell you in darkness, say in the light, and what you hear in the ear, proclaim it upon the rooftops.
And do not fear those who kill the body but cannot harm the soul. But rather, fear Him who is able to destroy both soul and body in hell.
Aren't two sparrows sold for a penny? And not one of them will fall to the ground without your Father's knowledge.
And as for you, even the hairs of your heads are all numbered.
Do not be afraid! You are better than many sparrows!
And whoever confesses Me before men, I will also confess him before My Father who is in heaven.
But whoever disowns me before people, I too will disown them before my Father who is in heaven.
Do not suppose that I have come to bring peace to the earth. I did not come to bring peace, but a sword.
And I have come to set a man against his father, a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
And the enemies of man are the people of his household.
Whoever loves a father or mother more than me does not deserve me, and whoever loves a son or daughter more than me does not deserve me.
And whoever does not take up their cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, and whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
He who accepts you accepts me, and he who accepts me accepts the One who sent me.
Whoever accepts a prophet in the name of a prophet will receive a prophet’s reward, and whoever accepts a righteous person in the name of a righteous person will receive a righteous person’s reward.
“And whoever gives one of these little ones even a cup of cold water in the name of a disciple, truly I say to you, he will not lose his reward.”
John the Baptist and Jesus
And when Jesus had finished instructing His twelve disciples, He departed from there to teach and preach in their cities. Jesus and John the Baptist.
Now John, while in prison, heard about the deeds of the Christ and sent two of his disciples,
And he said to him, “Are you the one to come, or should we expect another?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “Go and tell John what you hear and see:
The blind see, and the lame walk, and the lepers are cleansed, and the deaf hear, and the dead rise, and the poor have the gospel preached to them.
And blessed is he who does not stumble.
And while these two went, Jesus began to say to the crowds about John: “What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed swaying in the wind?”
But what did you go out to see? A man clothed in soft garments? Behold, those who wear soft clothing are in the palaces of kings.
But what did you go out to see? Prophets? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
For this is the one about whom it is written: ‘Behold, I send my messenger ahead of you, who will prepare your way before you.’
Truly I say to you, among those born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist; but he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
And from the days of John the Baptist until now the kingdom of heaven suffers violence, and the violent take it by force.
For all the prophets and the Law prophesied until John.
And if you are willing to accept it, this is Elijah who is about to come.
He who has ears to hear, let him hear.
And to whom can this generation be likened? They are like children sitting in the marketplaces, calling out to their companions.
And they say: We played the tune for you, yet you didn't dance! We are for you, yet you didn't slap your cheeks [in mourning]!
For John came, neither eating nor drinking, and they say, ‘He has a demon.’
“The Son of Man has come to eat and drink, and they say, ‘Look, a glutton and a wine drinker, a friend of tax collectors and sinners!’ Yet wisdom is justified by all her children.” Woe to you, towns that did not repent!
Then he began to rebuke the cities that had made the most of his power, because they had not repented.
Woe to you, Chorazin! Woe to you, Bethsaida! For if mighty works had been done in Sodom and Gomorrah, they would have remained until this day. But I tell you that it will be more bearable on that day for Sodom and Gomorrah than for you.
But I say to you that Tyre and Sidon will have a more favorable standing on the day of judgment than you.
And you, Capernaum, exalted to heaven! You will be brought down to Hades. For if the mighty works done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until today.
But I say to you that the land of Sodom will have a more favorable state on the Day of Judgment than you.” Relief for the weary.
At that time Jesus answered and said, “I thank you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, that you have hidden these things from the wise and understanding and revealed them to little children.”
Yes, Father, for so it was your will.
Everything that I have, I received from my Father, and no one knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone know the Father except the Son and anyone to whom the Son chooses to reveal Him.
Come to me, all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and humble in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
“Because my light is Niri and my burden is light.”
Lord of Saturday
At that time Jesus went on the Sabbath through the grainfields, and his disciples became hungry and began to pluck heads of grain and to eat.
Now the Pharisees saw that He and His disciples were doing things that were not lawful on the Sabbath, and they said to Him, “Behold, Your disciples do what is not lawful to do on the Sabbath!”
And He said to them, “Have you not read what David did when he was hungry, he and those with him?”
How did he enter the house of God and eat the bread of the presentation which was not permitted for him or those with him to eat, but only for the priests?
Or have you not read in the Law that the priests in the temple profane the Sabbath and are blameless?
But I tell you, something greater than the temple is here!
If you knew what it is: I want mercy, not sacrifice, you would not have condemned the innocent!
“For the Son of Man is Lord of the Sabbath as well.”
Then He departed from there and came to their assembly,
And if a man has a withered hand, they questioned Him, saying, “Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?” that they might accuse Him.
Then He said to them, “Which of you, if your sheep falls into a pit on the Sabbath, will not seize it and lift it out?”
So man is indeed better than the sheep! If doing good resolves in the spotlight!
Then He said to the man, “Extend your hand.” So he extended it, and it became sound like the other. A youth blessed by God.
And when the Pharisees went out, they conferred together about how to destroy him.
And Jesus taught and departed from there. And a great multitude followed Him, and He healed them all.
And he instructed them not to reveal it.
That what was spoken through the prophet Isaiah might be fulfilled:
Behold my son, whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights. I will put my spirit upon him, and he will proclaim justice to the nations.
He does not argue or shout, and no one hears his voice in the streets.
A bruised reed He will not break, and a smoldering wick He will not quench, until He brings forth justice to victory.
And in His name, the hope of the nations is.” Jesus and Beelzebul.
Then they brought to him a deaf-mute blind man, and He healed him, so that the blind man spoke and saw.
And all the assembly was amazed and said, “Is this not the son of David?”
Now the Pharisees, when they had heard, said, “This man does not cast out demons except by Beelzebul, the prince of demons.”
And Jesus knowing their thoughts said to them, “Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation, and every city or house divided against itself does not stand.”
If Satan casts out Satan, he is divided against himself. How then can his kingdom stand?
And if I by Beelzebul cast out devils, by whom do your children cast them out? therefore they shall be your judges!
But if it is by the Spirit of God that I drive out demons, then the kingdom of God has come upon you!
Or how can anyone enter a strong man’s house and plunder his goods, unless he first subdues the strong man—and then he plunders his house?
He who is not with me is against me, and he who does not gather with me scatters.
Therefore I say to you, every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven to people, but the blasphemy against the Spirit will not be forgiven to people.
And whoever says a word against the Son of Man, it will be forgiven him, but whoever speaks against the Holy Spirit, it will not be forgiven him, neither in this world nor in the world to come.
Make the tree good, and its fruit good, or make the tree bad, and its fruit bad, for you know the tree from its fruit.
You sons of vipers! How can you speak good things while you are evil? For the mouth speaks from the abundance of the heart.
A good person out of the treasure of goodness in his heart produces good, and an evil person out of the treasure of evil produces evil.
But I tell you that every idle word that people speak, they shall give account of it in the day of judgment.
For by your words you are justified, and by your words you are condemned.” Verse from Jonah.
Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered, saying, “Teacher, we want to see a sign from you.”
And he answered and said to them, “A wicked and adulterous generation seeks a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah the prophet.”
For as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the whale, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.
The men of Nineveh will stand in judgment with this generation and condemn it, for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here!
The Queen of Sheba will perform a duty in the religion with this generation and establish it, for she came from the farthest reaches of the earth to hear the wisdom of Solomon, and behold, one greater than Solomon is here!
And when the unclean spirit goes out of a man, it passes through dry places, seeking rest, and finds none.
Then he says, “I will return to my house from which I came.” But when he arrives, he finds it empty, swept, and decorated.
Then he goes and takes with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, and they enter and dwell there, and the last state of that person is worse than his first!” Thus it will also be to this wicked generation.” Doing the will of God.
And while He was speaking to the crowds, behold, His mother and brothers stood outside, seeking to speak to Him.
Then one said to Him, “Behold, Your mother and brothers are standing outside, seeking to speak with You.”
Then he answered and said to the one who spoke to Him, “Who is My mother, and who are My brothers?”
Then he stretched out his hand toward his disciples and said: “Here are my mother and my brothers.”
For whoever does the will of my Father who is in heaven is my brother and sister and mother.”
Like the sower and its interpretation.
On that day, Jesus went out of the house and sat by the sea.
So a large crowd gathered around him, so much so that he got into a boat and sat in it, while the whole crowd stood on the shore.
So he spoke to them at length in parables, saying: “Behold, the sower went out to sow,
And as he was sowing, some fell by the wayside, and the birds came and ate it.
And another fell on rocky places, where it did not have much soil, and it sprang up at once, because it had no depth of soil.
But when the sun rose, it was scorched, and having no root, it withered.
And another fell on thorns, and the thorns grew up and choked it.
And another fell on good ground and gave fruit, some a hundredfold, and some sixtyfold, and some thirtyfold.
"He who has ears to hear, let him hear."
Then the disciples came and asked Him, “Why do You speak to them in parables?”
So he answered and said to them, “Because it has been given to you to know the mysteries of the kingdom of heaven, but it has not been given to them.
And whoever has will be given and increased, but from whoever does not have, even what he has will be taken away.
For this reason I speak to them in parables: because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear nor understand.
Thus is fulfilled the prophecy of Isaiah which says: “You will be hearing, but not understanding; and you will be seeing, but not perceiving.”
For this people’s heart has grown callous, and their ears are hard of hearing, and their eyes they have closed, lest they should see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their heart and turn, and I would heal them.
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
For truly I say to you, that many prophets and righteous men have desired to see what you see, but have not seen it, and to hear what you hear, but have not heard it.
So listen, you, like the sower:
Anyone who hears the word of the kingdom and does not understand it comes and the evil one snatches away what was sown in their heart. This is the one sown along the path.
And the one sown on rocky ground is the one who hears the word and immediately receives it with joy,
But it has no origin in itself, but is temporary. So, if hardship or persecution occurs for the sake of the word, it immediately falters.
And the one sown among thorns is he who hears the word, and these are the ones who are worldly, and the deceitfulness of riches chokes the word, and it becomes unfruitful.
But the seed sown on good soil is the one who hears the word and understands it. This is the one who produces a harvest, some a hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty.” Like wheat and weeds.
Then he told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field.”
And while people were sleeping, his enemy came and sowed weeds among the wheat and went away.
And when the plants grew and bore fruit, then the weeds also appeared.
Then the slaves of the master of the house came and said to him, “Sir, did you not sow good seed in your field? Where then did the weeds come from?”
And he said to them: A man, an enemy, did this. And the slaves said to him: Do you want us to go and gather it?
He said: “No! lest you pull up the wheat with the tares when you gather them.”
“Let both grow together until the harvest. And at harvest time I will tell the harvesters, ‘First gather the weeds and tie them in bundles to be burned, but gather the wheat into my barn.’” Like a mustard seed and like yeast.
Then he told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that a man took and planted in his field,
It is the smallest of all seeds. But when it grows, it is the largest of plants, becoming a tree so that birds of the sky come and nest in its branches.”
He told them another parable: “The kingdom of heaven is like leaven that a woman took and hid in three measures of flour, till it was all leavened.”
All this Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables, and without a parable he did not speak to them.
In order that what was spoken might be fulfilled by the prophet who said: “I will open my mouth in parables, and utter things hidden since the foundation of the world.” An explanation of the parable of the wheat and the tares.
Then Jesus sent the crowds away and went into the house. His disciples came to him, saying, “Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field.”
Then he answered and said to them, “The sower is the son of man.
And the field is the world. And the good seed is the children of the kingdom. And the tares are the children of the evil one.
And the enemy who sowed it is Satan. And the harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are the angels.
Just as the chaff is gathered and burned with fire, so it will be at the end of this world:
The Son of Man will send his angels, and they will gather out of his kingdom all causes of stumbling and all evildoers,
And they will be cast into the fire. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.
Then the righteous will shine like the sun in the kingdom of their Father. Whoever has ears, let them hear. Examples of the treasure, the pearl, and the net.
Also, the kingdom of heaven is like a treasure hidden in a field, which a man found and hid, and in his joy he goes and sells all that he has and buys that field.
Also, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant seeking good pearls,
So when he found one pearl of great value, he went and sold all he had and bought it.
Also, the kingdom of heaven is like a net cast into the sea, gathering fish of every kind.
And when they were full, they hauled them up on the shore and sat down and gathered the good fish into containers, but the bad they threw outside.
Thus it will be at the end of the world: the angels will come and separate the wicked from among the righteous,
And they will be thrown into the fire. There will be weeping and gnashing of teeth.”
Jesus said to them, “Do you understand all this?” They replied, “Yes, Lord.”
So he said to them: “Therefore every scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a householder who brings out of his treasure things new and old.”
And when Jesus had finished these parables, he departed thence. Nazareth rejects Jesus.
And when He came to his hometown, He was teaching in their synagogue, so that they were astonished and said, “Where did this man get this wisdom and these mighty works?”
Isn't this the son of the carpenter? Isn't his mother's name Mary, and aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? Then where did he get all of this?
So they stumbled over him. And Jesus said to them, “A prophet is not without honor except in his hometown and in his own household.”
And not many forces were made there due to their lack of faith.
The beheading of John the Baptist
At that time Herod the tetrarch heard of the fame of Jesus,
Then he said to his servants, “This is John the Baptist risen from the dead! And therefore miracles are being done by him.”
Now Herod had arrested John and bound him in prison for the sake of Herodias, the wife of Philip his brother.
For John had been telling him, “It is not lawful for you.”
And when he wanted to kill him, he feared the people, for they regarded him as a prophet.
Then when Herod’s birthday came, the daughter of Herodias danced in the midst and pleased Herod.
Then he promised her with an oath that whatever she asked for, he would give it to her.
And as she had learned from her mother, she said, “Give me here on a platter the head of John the Baptist.”
And the king was greatly distressed. But for the sake of the oaths and those who were reclining with him, he commanded that it be given.
So he sent and had John the Baptist beheaded in prison.
So he brought his head on a platter and gave it to the girl, and she brought it to her mother.
Then his disciples came forward, took the body and buried it. Afterward they came and told Jesus. The feeding of the five thousand men.
And when Jesus heard this, He departed from there by boat to a deserted place by Himself. But when the crowds heard it, they followed Him on foot from the towns.
And when Jesus went out He saw a great crowd, and He had compassion on them and healed their sick.
And when evening came, his disciples came to him, saying, “This is a deserted place, and the hour is already late. Send the crowds away so they can go to the villages to buy themselves food.”
And Jesus said to them, “They do not need to go. You give them something to eat.”
And they said to him, “We have here only five loaves and two fish.”
And he said: “Bring it here to me.”
Then he ordered the crowds to sit down on the grass. Taking the five loaves and the two fish, he looked up to heaven, and blessed and broke the loaves, and gave them to the disciples, and the disciples to the crowds.
So they all ate and were satisfied. Then they collected what remained of the broken pieces into twelve baskets, full.
And those who ate were about five thousand men, not counting women and children. The miracle of walking on water.
And immediately Jesus made the disciples get into the boat and go before him to the other side, to Bethsaida, while he dismissed the crowd.
And after dismissing the crowds, He went up onto the mountain by Himself to pray. When evening came, He was there alone.
And as for the ship, it was already in the midst of the sea, tormented by the waves, because the wind was against it.
And in the fourth watch of the night Jesus came to them, walking on the sea.
And when the disciples saw Him walking on the sea, they were troubled, saying, “It is a ghost!” And they cried out in fear!
And immediately Jesus spoke to them, saying, “Take courage! It is I. Do not be afraid.”
Then Peter answered Him and said, “Lord, if it is You, command me to come to You on the water.”
Then He said, “Come.” And Peter got out of the boat and walked on the water and came to Jesus.
But when he saw that the storm was growing, he became afraid. And as he began to sink, he cried out, “Lord, save me!”
Then Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying to him, “O you of little faith, why did you doubt?”
And when they boarded the ship, the wind subsided.
And those in the ship came and prostrated themselves to Him, saying, “Truly, You are the Son of God!”
And when they had crossed over, they came to the land of Gennesaret.
So they recognized the men of that place. And they sent to all the region surrounding it and brought to him all the sick,
And they asked Him that they might only touch the fringe of His garment. And as many as touched it were made well.
The pure and the impure.
Then came to Jesus scribes and Pharisees from Jerusalem, saying:
Why do your students go beyond imitating the elders—they don’t wash their hands when they eat bread?
And he answered and said to them, “And you also, why do you transgress the commandment of God for the sake of your tradition?”
Indeed, God has commanded, saying: Honor your father and your mother, and whoever reviles a father or a mother should die the death.
And as for you, you say, ‘Whatever benefit I might derive from you is dedicated as a gift to God.’ Thus, he does not honor his father or his mother.
You have nullified the decree of Allah because of your adherence to tradition!
Hypocrites! Well did Isaiah prophesy of you, saying:
He draws near to this people with his mouth and honors me with his lips, but his heart is far from me.
And they worship Me in vain, while they know these are teachings—commands of men.”
Then he called the crowd and said to them, “Listen and understand.”
“It is not what enters the mouth that defiles a person, but what comes out of the mouth; this is what defiles a person.”
Then His disciples came forward and said to Him, “Do You know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this saying?”
And he answered and said, “Every plant that my heavenly Father has not planted will be uprooted.”
Leave them. They are blind leaders of the blind. And if a blind man leads a blind man, both will fall into a pit.”
Then Peter answered and said to Him, “Explain this parable to us.”
Then Jesus said, “Do you still not understand?”
Do you still not understand that everything that enters the mouth goes into the stomach and then out into the latrine?
And as for what comes out of the mouth, it comes from the heart, and that is what defiles a person.
For from the heart come evil thoughts: murder, adultery, sexual immorality, theft, false testimony, blasphemy.
“These are the things that defile a person: but eating with unwashed hands does not defile a person.” The Canaanite woman’s faith.
Then Jesus went away from there and withdrew to the region of Tyre and Sidon.
And a Canaanite woman from that region came out and cried to him, saying, “Have mercy on me, Lord, Son of David! My daughter is severely possessed.”
But he did not answer her a word. Then his disciples came forward and asked him, saying, “Send her away, for she cries out after us!”
And he answered and said, “I was not sent except to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.”
So she came and prostrated herself before Him, saying: “O Lord, help me!”
And she answered and said, “It is not good to take the children’s bread and throw it to the dogs.”
And she said: “Yes, sir! And dogs also eat from the crumbs that fall from their masters’ table!”
Then Jesus answered and said to her, “Woman, great is your faith! Let it be to you as you desire.” And her daughter was healed from that hour. Feeding the Four Thousand Men
Then Jesus went from there and came to the shore of the Sea of Galilee, and went up on the mountain and sat there.
So a large crowd came to him, including those who were lame, blind, paralyzed, and mute, and many others. They laid them at Jesus’ feet, and he healed them.
So the crowds were amazed when they saw the mute speaking, the crippled healed, the lame walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified the God of Israel.
Now Jesus called his disciples and said to them, “I have compassion for the crowd, because they have been with me for three days now and have nothing to eat. And I don’t want to send them away hungry, lest they faint on the way.”
Then his disciples said to him, “Where shall we get enough bread in the wilderness to feed so large a crowd?”
Then Jesus asked them, “How many loaves of bread do you have?” They replied, “Seven—and a few small fish.”
And he commanded the crowds to recline on the ground,
And he took the seven loaves and the fish, and gave thanks and broke them and gave to his disciples, and the disciples gave to the crowd.
So they all ate and were satisfied. Then they collected what was left of the broken pieces—seven baskets full.
And those who ate were four thousand men, not counting women and children.
Then he dismissed the crowds, went aboard the ship, and came to the vicinity of Magdala.
Request a verse.
Then the Pharisees and Sadducees came to Him, testing Him, and asked Him to show them a sign from heaven.
And he answered and said to them, “When evening comes, you say, ‘It will be fair weather,’ for the sky is red.
And in the morning: Today is winter because the sky is flushed with anger. O hypocrites! You know how to discern the face of the sky, but you cannot discern the signs of the times!
“A wicked and adulterous generation seeks a sign, and no sign will be given to it except the sign of Jonah the prophet.” Then he left them and went away. The leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
And when His disciples came to the other side, they forgot to take bread.
And Jesus said to them, “Beware and watch out for the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”
So they thought to themselves, saying, “We have not taken any bread.”
Then Jesus asked them, “Why are you discussing among yourselves that you have no bread? Do you still not understand?”
Even now do you not understand? Do you not remember the five loaves for the five thousand, and how many baskets you took?
And seven loaves of the four thousand, and how many baskets did you take?
"Don't you understand? I wasn't talking about bread. I was warning you to beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.”
Then they understood that He hadn't warned them to beware of the yeast of bread, but of the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter Confesses the Messiah.
And when Jesus came into the regions of Caesarea Philippi, He asked His disciples, saying, “Who do people say that I, the Son of Man, am?”
And they said, “John the Baptist, and others, Elijah, and others, Jeremiah or one of the prophets.”
He asked them, “But what about you? Who do you say that I am?”
Then Simon Peter answered and said, “You are the Christ, the Son of the living God!”
And Jesus answered and said to him, “Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah, for flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but My Father who is in heaven.”
And I say to you also, you are Peter, and on this rock I will build My church, and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
and I will give you the keys of the kingdom of heaven, and whatever you bind on earth will be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth will be loosed in heaven.”
Then He warned His disciples not to tell anyone that He was the Christ. Jesus foretells His death and resurrection.
From that time Jesus began to show to his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and be raised on the third day.
Then Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him, saying, “Far be it from you, Lord! This shall not be!”
Then he turned and said to Peter, “Get behind me, Satan! You are a stumbling block to me, because you do not have in mind the things of God, but the things of men.”
Then Jesus said to his disciples, “If anyone wants to come after me, he must deny himself, take up his cross, and follow me.”
Whoever desires to save his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
For what profiteth it a man, if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his own soul? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?
For the Son of Man will come in the glory of his Father with his angels, and then he will repay each person according to what they have done.
Truly, I say to you, there are some standing here who will not taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom.”
Theophany
And after six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain by themselves.
And His appearance changed before them, and His face shone like the sun, and His garments became white as light.
And Moses and Elijah appeared to them, talking with Him.
Then Peter said to Jesus, “Lord, it is good that we are here! If you wish, I will make three shelters: one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah.”
And while he was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and a voice from the cloud said, “This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased; listen to him.”
And when the disciples heard this, they fell on their faces and were very afraid.
Then Jesus came and touched them, saying, “Get up, and do not be afraid.”
So they lifted their eyes and saw no one except Jesus alone.
And as they were coming down the mountain, Jesus instructed them, saying, “Tell no one what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead.”
And his disciples asked him, saying, “Why do the scribes say that Elijah must come first?”
And Jesus answered and said to them, “Indeed, Elijah is coming first and will restore all things.”
But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and they did not recognize him. Instead, they did whatever they pleased with him. So also the Son of Man will suffer from them.”
Then the disciples understood that He had spoken to them about John the Baptist. Healing a boy possessed by a demon.
And when they came to the gathering, a man approached him, falling on his knees before him.
And saying, “Lord, have mercy on my son, for he is an epileptic and suffers terribly, and often falls into the fire and often into the water.”
And I brought him to your disciples, and they could not heal him.”
And Jesus answered and said, “O faithless and twisted generation, how long shall I be with you? How long shall I bear with you? Bring him here to me!”
Then Jesus rebuked him, and the demon left the boy. And the boy was healed from that hour.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, “Why could we not cast it out?”
